window.HB_SEARCH = [{"url":"adr/adr-004-a1.html","title":"ADR-004-A1: only nava_audit_reader reads the raw diff","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-04, by the Solution Architect after all three reviewers approved. Amendment to ADR-004 : amends R7, R12, R14, CI-A3 and the audit_log_select and entity_definition_versions_select policies. Normative text: the A1 record ; explainer: A1-explained.html . Corrected on one point by ADR-004-E8 (the reader's column list). Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Options considered\", \"Accepted risks\". Problem ADR-004 masks secret pointers in audit_log.diff when it is read, and only internal/audit/read runs the masker. But nava_app also held column SELECT on diff and diff_salt , so any nava_app query elsewhere returned the raw diff. Row-level security limits rows, not columns, and the static guard reads Go source, so it cannot see SQL built at runtime. Nothing read the diff outside the reader, so this closed a latent risk, not a live exploit. Decision A dedicated login role, nava_audit_reader , is the only runtime role that can read audit_log.diff and diff_salt . It has its own PgBouncer login and its own connection pool, held by internal/audit/read . nava_app loses both column grants. The role has SELECT only, on listed audit_log columns and on entity_definition_versions (definition_id, schema) ; no memberships, no other grants. audit_log_select is limited to nava_worker , nava_erasure and nava_audit_reader ; nava_app gets row access only through the generated writer policy. The masker fails closed: if a definition has no visible version row, the request fails with a 500 and returns no diff. The pool is capped at 5 connections, in PgBouncer and in the Go pool. The reader's DSN is read only by the config loader. Record history is two transactions: nava_app checks the record first (same 404 for missing, unreadable and other-tenant), then the reader pool reads entries. CI-A3 gains a catalogue check that no other runtime role holds SELECT on diff or diff_salt , on the table or any partition, and that the policy's role list is exact. The earlier static guard stays as a second layer. The role, the revoke and the reader pool land in one PR, because between them the audit endpoints would return 500. Options that lost A narrow SECURITY DEFINER function (breaks the no-definer-function rule); SET ROLE (needs a role membership, also forbidden); masking in a database view or function; keeping only the static guard; granting the reader records ; a CONNECTION LIMIT instead of a pool cap. Accepted risks Id Risk AR-TICK-549-01 (Low) Code inside the core process that deliberately opens a second pool with the reader DSN reads raw diffs. The guard and review cover it. AR-TICK-549-02 (Info) One more pool: up to 5 server connections per PgBouncer instance."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e1.html","title":"ADR-004-E1: cascade audit entries and diff_truncated","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED (rev 2), 2026-11-02, by the Solution Architect; nothing contested. Erratum to ADR-004 : replaces the R2 action list and the R1 envelope list, amends R23 and adds acceptance test CI-A17. Normative text: the E1 record ; explainer: E1-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\" items 1 and 2, \"Consequences\". Problem Two rows required by the cascade rules (ADR-001-A5 and the records-path plan) could not be expressed in ADR-004's vocabulary. Separately, a diff over 64 KB had no way to say it was cut. Decision Item 1, cascade entries (new R2a). action gains cascade_cycle and cascade_too_deep ; subject_type gains cascade_key . A cycle entry is written in the write's transaction, once per suppressed start. A too-deep entry is written on the pipeline's own transaction runner and commits independently of the rejected command. The cascade key is a fixed-namespace UUIDv5 over tenant, origin, record and definition, computed in Go by both the writer and the verifier. At most one cycle entry per origin, record and definition per write, so the unique key cannot roll back the user's write. Neither entry ever carries the attempted record data or an idempotency key. Item 2, diff_truncated . The envelope gains a boolean diff_truncated , always emitted, inside the chain, so flipping it is detectable. Past 64 KB the stored diff keeps whole RFC 6902 operations only; secrets are checked on the full diff before the cut. audit/erase entries are never truncated: the cap is at most 1,500 entry ids per erase entry, and an oversize one is refused, so erasure cannot silently leave entries that verify as tampered . No migration or backfill was needed; the table was empty when this was accepted."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e2.html","title":"ADR-004-E2: verifiability is computed, the trailer must agree","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-02 (rev 2), by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's re-check; nothing contested. Erratum to ADR-004 R20 and to the audit export format ( docs/api/audit-export-v1.md ). Normative text: the E2 record ; explainer: E2-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Outcome table\". Problem R20 said an export made with stubs=false always reports verifiable: false . That judged some honest files tampered , for example a filtered window whose last entries were excluded. Decision The header's stubs field is the request, not a claim. It only decides whether every seq in the range must appear (a stubs: true file with a gap is tampered ). Only the trailer's verifiable must equal the computed value; a disagreement is tampered ( verifiable_flag_mismatch ). \"Computed\" includes the tail: the range is contiguous only if the anchor is present, no seq is skipped, and the last range line is last_seq . An honest stubs: false export that excluded the tail is unverifiable , not tampered . The exporter still streams: it counts as it goes and writes verifiable = (range non-empty) and (count + stub_count = last_seq - anchor_seq) . The verifier's reason codes become a closed list. Not a breaking change to v1: every file that passed still passes, and no exporter had shipped. The format stays nava.audit.export/v1 . A binding outcome table in the record maps header stubs , range shape and trailer flag to pass , unverifiable or tampered ."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e3.html","title":"ADR-004-E3: the commit-check probe is planned on every call","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-03, by the Solution Architect after all reviewers approved; the CTO ruled on one trade-off. Erratum to ADR-004 decision 2, R28 and CI-A3. Normative text: the E3 record ; explainer: E3-explained.html . Its CI verdict rule is later changed by E4 . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Budget\". Problem The commit-check trigger probes audit_log once per subject at COMMIT. Under PostgreSQL's default plan caching, a pooled backend switches to a cached generic plan that is a sequential scan on a young partition and keeps it. A later import of thousands of subjects then costs quadratic time at COMMIT (2.9 s at 4,000 subjects), and the single-subject path decays too. Decision The commit-check function carries SET plan_cache_mode = force_custom_plan , so every probe is planned with its own values and no plan outlives the call. A session setting cannot override it. The probe reads only plpgsql locals, never NEW.\u003ccol\u003e , so bound values appear as constants in the plan. CI-A3 checks the function's catalogue settings for the new option. The performance tests check the plan that actually runs at COMMIT, not a prepared generic plan. The budget row of 100 µs per call is replaced by two rows measured against 38 partitions: single subject must stay flat across sizes (max/min under 1.5) and import per probe has its own ceiling. Same-run ratio limits guard against runner noise. Rollout is one normal migration, before any table attaches the trigger. Scope is one function, one migration and one catalogue check; no schema, grant, policy, role or database setting changes."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e4.html","title":"ADR-004-E4: commit-check ceilings are report-only in CI","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-03, by the Solution Architect, wording the CTO's decision (Option B with four conditions); nothing contested. Erratum to ADR-004-E3 's Budget section. It is numbered E4 because E3 was already taken by the erratum it corrects. Normative text: the E4 record ; explainer: E4-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Verdict table\". Problem On one commit, the same CI job read 129.5 µs and 190.9 µs on two GitHub-hosted runners against a 175 µs ceiling, with the same-run ratios unchanged. Absolute ceilings measured on shared hosts produced red builds that were not regressions. Decision The commit-check-budget job keeps measuring both rows as E3 defines them. Only the verdict rule changes. Absolute ceilings (per call and per import probe) become review thresholds: going over emits a warning annotation and the job stays green. The job fails on any of: a missing or non-positive measurement, a same-run ratio out of range, or a clause arm that is not flat across sizes. Every run logs its numbers and the host CPU model, and writes both rows to the step summary. Baselines in docs/budgets.md record the CPU model. The old \"re-run\" verdict is removed; that case is now a warning. Accepted risks E4-R1: a regression that slows the clause and the same-run reference equally keeps the ratios in range and passes. The cue to look was later reworded by ADR-004-E6 ."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e5.html","title":"ADR-004-E5: no FK actions on audited tables, no secrets in records","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-04, by the Solution Architect. Contested points were settled by CTO tie-breaks. Erratum to ADR-004 : adds rule R29, amends R6 for records and CI-A3. Normative text: the E5 record ; explainer: E5-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\" D1 to D11. Problem records.assignment_group_id was ON DELETE SET NULL from groups . When group sync deleted a group, PostgreSQL itself rewrote the records as the table owner, bypassing the write pipeline: no version bump, no audit entry, no outbox event. The commit-check trigger refused that write at COMMIT, so group sync, deprovisioning included, stalled whenever a record named the deleted group. Decision Tombstone groups. groups gains deleted_at . Sync never deletes a group row; a returning group is revived on the same platform id. The sync role loses DELETE on groups . R29. Every foreign key referencing an audited table must use NO ACTION or RESTRICT; CI checks it from the catalogue. records_assignment_group_fkey becomes RESTRICT . Readers. Sync's own diff read and the policy subquery see tombstoned rows; user-facing readers (such as /me group paths) filter them out. Admin and auditor pointers are deleted in the same transaction when their group is tombstoned, with matching narrow DELETE grants and policies. Pipeline. A write that changes assignment_group_id to a tombstoned group is refused with 422 assignment_group_removed ; an unchanged value passes. Records keep the reference and read models mark it removed: true . Database guard. A generated live column and composite foreign key make the database refuse members in a tombstoned group, for every role. No secrets in records. R6 applies to record payloads: a value at an x-nava-secret or writeOnly path is refused with 422 audit_secret_in_diff . There is no \"store and mask\" mode. Publishing a record definition that requires a marked property is refused. Migration order: expand first, contract only after the new binaries run everywhere."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e6.html","title":"ADR-004-E6: the E4-R1 cue is a per-model 1.2x band","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-05, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect approved with nothing contested. Erratum to the accepted risk E4-R1 in ADR-004-E4 only. It is numbered E6 because E1 to E5 were taken. Normative text: the E6 record ; explainer: E6-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\". Problem E4's cue to look for a uniform slowdown was an absolute warning. That is always on for a slow CPU model (one model's import baseline is already over the threshold) and blind on a fast one (a slowdown must reach more than twice the baseline before any warning). A spike showed timings on one CPU model are stable (max/min under 1.2). Decision A row is off-baseline when the run's CPU model has a recorded baseline in docs/budgets.md and the row is more than 1.2 times that model's recorded value. E4-R1 is revisited on two off-baseline main runs in a row on a recorded model, or a baseline re-take more than 1.2 times the previous one. Runs on unrecorded models neither count nor break the streak. An over-threshold baseline alone is not a regression signal; it is annotated as a slow host when its ratios match the other models. The check stays manual, owned by the Postgres specialist. A baseline re-take is the median of at least three runs on that model. Nothing in the CI gate changes: no verdict, threshold or code."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e7.html","title":"ADR-004-E7: A1 books the 10 s interval when work is pending","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-07, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's critique and a CTO ruling on the path. It clarifies R16 of ADR-004 and changes nothing else; R-A2 is unchanged. Normative text: the E7 record ; explainer: E7-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Options considered\", \"Consequences\". Problem The sealer's first activity (A1) books the next run before the second (A2) seals. In the code, A1 used the interval that the previous run's A2 had stored, so every interval change landed one run late. After an idle period, entries written just after a run waited up to 300 s even though that run had sealed something, and a burst larger than one run's cap could wait about 600 s, breaking the documented bound and firing the sealing-lag alert on a healthy system. Decision R16's interval bullet is reworded: on the non-stale path and under the head lock, A1 checks whether the tenant has any unsealed entry. If so it books the next run 10 s ahead and stores 10 s; otherwise it books at the stored interval. A2 stores the interval for later runs (10 s if it sealed anything, otherwise doubled up to 300 s; unchanged on error). Bound, absent an A2 failure: an entry is sealed within the delay already booked when it was written (at most 300 s) plus one run's duration. A poison entry still blocks later entries; that is the failure-metric and chain:reseed path. Options that lost Rewording R16 to match the code and relaxing the bound to about 600 s; running A2 before A1 (ends the lineage on a crash); moving the next run's available_at forward from A2 (a new sealer write path and grant). Consequences No Temporal determinism impact: the change sits inside the A1 activity, so recorded histories replay as they are. Idempotency is unchanged."},{"url":"adr/adr-004-e8.html","title":"ADR-004-E8: the reader also reads the chain columns","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-08, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's critique and Security's approval on the PR; not contested. Erratum to ADR-004-A1 A1-1 and A1-7 only. It corrects an omission and changes no rule. Normative text: the E8 record ; explainer: E8-explained.html . Paraphrases: \"Context\", \"Decision\", \"Why these three columns are safe\". Problem A1 puts the export pages on the nava_audit_reader pool, and the export format needs the chain links and the erasure reference. But A1-1's column list omitted prev_hash , entry_hash and erasure_ref , so an export page failed with a permission error. A1 also did not say where the export's head sequence and hash come from. Decision The reader's audit_log grant gains prev_hash , entry_hash and erasure_ref , 30 columns in all. txid stays out. The three columns reveal nothing new: the hashes are derived from columns the reader already reads and are published by the export, and the erasure reference equals the matching erase entry's subject id. The head read for an export is the lease statement itself on nava_app , returning the head seq and entry_hash ; zero rows means 409 export_in_progress . The reader gets no grant on audit_chain_head . The lease transaction commits before the first page is read, so the head row lock is never held while paging."},{"url":"adr/adr-004.html","title":"ADR-004: one audit log, sealed by a per-tenant hash chain","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-02, by the Solution Architect after all three reviewers approved in round 2 (no dissent recorded). Normative text: ADR-004-audit-log.md ; explainer: ADR-004-explained.html . Supporting material: rfc/ , spikes/ . Amendment and errata: A1 , E1 , E2 , E3 , E4 , E5 , E6 , E7 , E8 . Each section names the ADR heading it paraphrases. Problem Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"Consequences\". The product needs one history of record changes and configuration changes that a buyer can export and check for tampering, under any filter, without trusting the operator's word. Decision Paraphrases: \"Decision\" 1 to 6. One log. A single append-only table, audit_log , with three domains: record , config and audit (exports, erasures, reseeds). One writer ( audit.Append , inside the caller's transaction), one reader and one export. The database checks the same-transaction rule. Every audited table gets a deferred constraint trigger. At COMMIT it requires an entry for the same tenant, transaction and subject. A raw write that skips audit.Append fails. Tamper evidence. A per-tenant hash chain, entry_hash = SHA-256(prev_hash ‖ envelope_sha256) , ordered by seq . A self-rescheduling sealer assigns seq asynchronously; writers never wait on it. Exports verify offline under any filter. Entries a filter excludes appear as hash-only stubs, and the range is anchored one entry before the window. The verifier ships as nava audit verify \u003cfile\u003e . Erasure is one of exactly two transitions allowed on a written row, and only the nava_erasure role may make it. The chain covers a salted diff hash, so it still verifies after erasure. S8's published versions are backfilled into the log by two migrations; they are not exposed through a view. Rules that matter most Paraphrases: \"Rules\", R1 to R28. Area What the ADR fixes Content (R1 to R4) The envelope columns, closed vocabularies for action , source and subject_type , versioned configuration with content hashes, and how AI and change-set actions are attributed. Secrets and masks (R5 to R8) One diff walker. A secret at an x-nava-secret or writeOnly path is rejected at write time. Masks are applied at read time. Reading needs the standalone audit.read or audit.export permission. Isolation and immutability (R9 to R12) FORCE row-level security, no DELETE grant for any role, INSERT asserted (never rewritten) by trigger, exactly two UPDATE transitions (seal, erase), writer grants generated from db/audited-tables.yaml . Chain and sealer (R13 to R18) The hash definition and golden vectors, one audit_chain_head row per tenant, seq rules, the self-rescheduling sealer, and chain:reseed (409 chain_alive unless the chain looks dead for 10 minutes). Export (R19 to R22) One export per tenant at a time, the anchored range, paging, JSONL and CSV formats with formula-prefix protection. Erasure (R23, R24) The erase step for Epic 30 and the verifier outcomes for erased entries ( tampered or pending unless a chained erase entry names them). Backfill and storage (R25 to R28) The order of migrations A and B, the access path for the backfill, lock order, and the partitioned key shape. Operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses listAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries The tenant's audit timeline 200, 400, 401, 403, 429, 500, 501 exportAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries:export Download a verifiable JSONL export of the audit log 200, 400, 401, 403, 409, 429, 500, 501 reseedAuditChain POST /api/v1/audit/chain:reseed Restart a dead audit seal lineage 200, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 429, 500, 501 Options that lost Paraphrases: \"Rejected options\". Separate record and configuration logs; a view over S8 versions instead of a backfill; chaining inside the writer's transaction; a shared seal key per minute (writers queued 3.9 to 4.0 s in two spikes); one seal start per transaction; an eternal per-tenant workflow; triggers that write the audit rows (they cannot know the actor or AI decision); jsonb diffs (the stored bytes would no longer match their hash); a single REPEATABLE READ export snapshot; external anchoring in the first milestone; a dedicated sealer role. Consequences Paraphrases: \"Consequences\". Each configuration epic registers its subject types and manifest rows. Entries carry sealed: false until the sealer ships. Sealing roughly doubles heap WAL on audit_log . The sealer needs an outbox producer entry point not tied to a record. Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"Accepted risks\". # Risk Revisit trigger R-A1 A consistent whole-chain rewrite by the owner is detected only against an export the tenant already holds. External anchoring was deferred. A regulated buyer asks for external attestation. R-A2 Sealing lag: about 10 s when the tenant is active, up to 300 s after idle. The audit_unsealed_oldest_seconds alert fires in production. R-A4 Commit-trigger, sealing-WAL and deferred-queue costs were unmeasured when accepted (spike S1). The S1 result. R-A5 Idle-tenant seal starts add Temporal load. Starts above 10% of Temporal capacity. R-A6 Migration B holds ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locks across its transaction. S8 volume at planning. (R8) A compromised worker credential can seal or insert for any tenant. Already accepted in ADR-001. The ADR-001 R8 trigger. Acceptance tests Paraphrases: \"Acceptance tests\", CI-A1 to CI-A16. They prove, among other things: no entry survives a rollback; a raw write to an audited table fails at COMMIT; read, export and reseed never cross tenants; export verification passes under every filter and fails on an edited byte, a deleted row or a deleted first entry; erasure verifies; the backfill leaves one entry per S8 version; a killed sealer is recovered by the next run."},{"url":"adr/adr-005.html","title":"ADR-005: record numbering","section":"adr","text":"Status on main : ACCEPTED , 2026-11-10, by the Solution Architect after the Technical Architect's critique and CTO rulings; no dissent. The ADR is accepted as a whole, but only part of it is built (see \"What is on main\"). Normative text: ADR-005-record-numbering.md ; explainer: ADR-005-explained.html . Supporting material: rfc/ , spikes/ . Delivery plan: plan-adr-005.md . Each section names the ADR heading it paraphrases. What is on main Part of the ADR State Per-type numbering_mode ( gaps default, gapless ) on entity definitions, and the allocator for both modes (story S10) Delivered on main . Scoped counters ( scope_key on record_counters , expand then contract migrations), release R1 (story S17) In progress, not on main . Number format tables, links to entity types, epochs, the admin API, formats rendered by the allocator, mandatory counter purge, and the contract migration (story S18) In progress, not on main . Field tokens (enum properties), immutable-field marker, coupled mask publish (story S19) In progress, not on main . Plugin-authored formats (story S20) Planned , parked until the plugin principal condition is met. Plugins that run code per create, or own the counter Deferred (per-create) and rejected (counter ownership). Until the scoped counters and formats ship, every type numbers with today's format, the type's prefix followed by seven digits, on the one counter row per tenant and type. Problem Paraphrases: \"Context\". Every record gets a human-facing number taken inside the create transaction, which keeps the counter row locked until the create commits. With 8 clients creating the same type in one tenant, counter wait p95 was 237 to 310 ms and create p95 264 to 408 ms against a 50 ms target. Tenants also want formats such as monthly or daily resets, or per-branch counters, and the format was fixed in code. Decision Paraphrases: \"Decision\", \"2. The allocator\". Allocate in its own tiny transaction (L2s) on a dedicated allocator pool, after authorisation and validation and before the create transaction, with synchronous_commit off for that transaction only. This is the path for gaps types, the default. A create that fails after allocation leaves a hole; gaps are never reclaimed. gapless types take their number last, inside the create transaction (L1), so a rolled-back create gives its number back. The 50 ms 8-client target is waived for those types only. Both paths upsert the same counter row, so changing a type's mode cannot produce a duplicate number; the change applies to creates that start afterwards. Why L2s cannot put one number on two records: a record's synchronous commit flushes all earlier WAL, including the counter advance, so a crash can lose only advances whose numbers never reached a committed record. Number formats (planned, S17 to S19): declarative templates that start with the type's {prefix} , with exactly one {seq:N} , date parts, and enum field codes; versioned tenant metadata linked to entity types by pinned version. Scoped counters (planned): record_counters gains scope_key and a period end, with an expand, deploy, contract migration. The default format keeps today's counter, so no existing number is migrated. Options that lost Paraphrases: \"1. Options considered\", \"6. Plugins\". Keeping the counter lock until commit (misses the target); allocating on the main pool before authorisation (a caller with no create permission could advance a counter); cached blocks of numbers (large gaps on restart, kept as a trigger for very hot scopes); assigning the number after commit; a plugin that owns the counter or runs inside the lock (rejected, I8). Accepted risks Paraphrases: \"8. Accepted risks\". Id Risk AR-TICK-653-01 (Low) Numbers issued before a field was masked keep showing that field's code. AR-TICK-653-02 (Low) A per-scope sequence reveals volume per scope, within the tenant only. AR-TICK-653-03 (Low) The no-duplicate guarantee assumes WAL replays as a prefix; logical replication on record_counters reopens the ADR. AR-TICK-653-04 (Info) The allocator pool adds 4 connections per API replica. (product) Gaps on gaps types after failed creates or a crash. A tenant with a legal gapless requirement sets numbering_mode = gapless . Still owed Paraphrases: \"10. Still owed\". A one-client create over HTTP at or under 50 ms on a quiet host, and the matching budget rows."},{"url":"adr/index.html","title":"Architecture decisions","section":"adr","text":"An ADR records one decision and is normative. An amendment ( A\u003cn\u003e ) changes a rule of its parent ADR; an erratum ( E\u003cn\u003e ) corrects or clarifies one. Each page here summarises its record and links the normative text, which always wins. Pages marked in-progress describe work that is not yet on main . Record Title Status Date ADR-001 Platform blueprint — invariants, D26, D6, D22, D28 (system actors) ACCEPTED 2026-10-15 ADR-001-A1 Temporal server v1.29.7 / api v1.53.0, the D6-K startup guard and the D9 workload allow-list ACCEPTED 2026-10-20 ADR-001-A2 Erratum E2 and amendment A2 to ADR-001-A1, closing system-workflow and task-queue reach via service certificates (F2) ACCEPTED 2026-10-23 ADR-001-A5 Amendment A5 to ADR-001 I9 and CI-10, a cascade cycle suppresses the re-start (one start per tenant, origin, record and definition) instead of rejecting the write ACCEPTED 2026-10-31 ADR-001-A6 Amendment A6 to ADR-001 Annex A.2 and A.3 — the nava_erasure role, onboarding seeds the audit sealer, and start rows not tied to a record ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-001-E1 command inspection for continue-as-new ACCEPTED (inherited) 2026-10-15 ADR-001-E3 Erratum E3 to ADR-001 Annex A.3, nava_onboard has SELECT on its own row, not only its own provisioning row ACCEPTED 2026-10-25 ADR-002 GET /api/v1/me exposes the caller's group paths, tenant-scoped and with the tenant prefix stripped ACCEPTED 2026-10-27 ADR-002-E1 Erratum E1 to ADR-002, a line-scoped nolint pinned by a CI gate replaces the api.go forbidigo exclusion ACCEPTED 2026-10-28 ADR-003 A recorded interpreter history is retired only by raising GetVersion's minSupported (call kept), with an append-only tombstone CI can check ACCEPTED 2026-11-01 ADR-004 One audit log for records and configuration, sealed by a per-tenant hash chain and exportable (CR-AUDIT-1) ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-A1 Amendment A1 to ADR-004: only nava_audit_reader can read audit_log.diff and diff_salt, through the masking reader's own pool ACCEPTED 2026-11-04 ADR-004-E1 Erratum E1 to ADR-004, the R2 vocabulary and key shape for the two cascade audit entries, and diff_truncated in the R1 envelope ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-E2 Erratum E2 to ADR-004 R20 and audit-export-v1 §3/§4: only the trailer's verifiable must equal the computed value; header stubs is the request ACCEPTED 2026-11-02 ADR-004-E3 Erratum E3 to ADR-004 decision 2: the commit-check probe is planned on every call (force_custom_plan), Finding 5 tests the plan that runs, and the budget is rebased ACCEPTED 2026-11-03 ADR-004-E4 Erratum E4 to ADR-004-E3 (budget): in CI the absolute commit-check ceilings are report-only; the job fails only on a same-run ratio, on flatness, or when nothing was measured ACCEPTED 2026-11-03 ADR-004-E5 Erratum E5 to ADR-004: no referential actions on audited tables (group sync tombstones groups), and no secrets in record payloads ACCEPTED 2026-11-04 ADR-004-E6 Erratum E6 to ADR-004-E4 (accepted risk E4-R1): the uniform-regression cue is a per-model 1.2x band around the recorded baseline, not the absolute review threshold ACCEPTED 2026-11-05 ADR-004-E7 Erratum E7 to ADR-004 R16: A1 applies the 10 s interval when the tenant has unsealed entries; A2 stores the interval for later runs ACCEPTED 2026-11-07 ADR-004-E8 Erratum E8 to ADR-004-A1 A1-1 and A1-7: the reader also reads prev_hash, entry_hash and erasure_ref; the R20 head read is nava_app's lease statement ACCEPTED 2026-11-08 ADR-005 Record numbering — a number allocator outside the create transaction (L2s), declarative number formats linked to entity types, scoped counters ACCEPTED 2026-11-10"},{"url":"benchmarks/index.html","title":"Benchmarks","section":"benchmarks","text":"TODO: the performance budgets and how they are measured. Section Budget Owner Commit-check trigger ( audit_commit_check , ADR-004 decision 2), single subject Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Commit-check trigger, import Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Migration lock holds Migration A/B tenant-lock hold (S8 backfill, audit_backfill_s8() ): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on tenants , entity_definition_versions and audit_log until COMMIT Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Migration lock holds Migration B lock hold (P5-32): ACCESS EXCLUSIVE on tenants , entity_definitions , entity_definition_versions , audit_log , audit_chain_head and outbox until COMMIT (R27) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Record history Record history length ( GET /api/v1/records/{entity}/{id}/history ) Principal Software Engineer Record numbering Counter wait, gaps type (L2s) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist Record numbering Create, 1 client, over HTTP Quality, Testing \u0026 CI Advisor Record numbering Create, 8 clients, gaps type Quality, Testing \u0026 CI Advisor Record numbering Waiver: create, 8 clients, gapless type (L1) Postgres \u0026 Data Modeling Specialist"},{"url":"database/index.html","title":"Database","section":"database","text":"TODO: tables by area, from the schema snapshot. Migrations Migration Purpose 00001_global_revokes.sql ADR-001 A.3 global settings. Runs as nava_migrator, the database owner. 00002_platform_tables.sql T09 (TICK-64): the platform tables of ADR-001 A.3, each under ENABLE + FORCE 00003_identity_tables.sql T16 (TICK-69): the identity tables group sync (T25) fills from Keycloak and 00004_tenant_email_domain.sql T18a (TICK-194): the email domains each tenant owns. InviteUser creates a 00005_purge_indexes.sql T13 follow-up (TICK-146): indexes for nava-purge's batch DELETEs 00006_pending_invite.sql T252a (TICK-254): the admins a tenant has invited who have not signed in 00007_email_domain_release.sql TICK-280 (follow-up from TICK-276 item 1, AR-036): nava_onboard may 00008_outbox_payload_references.sql T32c (TICK-214), CI-7 finding T32b-1 (docs/security/temporal-ci6.md): an 00009_outbox_payload_keys.sql T32f (TICK-240), CI-7 finding T32b-1 remainder (docs/security/temporal-ci6.md): 00010_principal_tombstone.sql TICK-220 (T25 follow-up): a principal who leaves the Keycloak directory is 00011_principal_tombstone_validate.sql TICK-220: validates 00010's principals_tombstone_check in its own 00012_drop_principals_delete_policy.sql TICK-265 (contract step of TICK-220): group sync tombstones a principal 20261028175712_idempotency_lease.sql T22a (TICK-212): a lease nava_app can extend, so a takeover of an abandoned 20261031150000_entity_definitions.sql P3-02 (TICK-340): entity types and their published, immutable versions 20261031160000_tenant_admin_group.sql P3-04 (TICK-342, PLAN-003 D-3-3): the platform id of each tenant's admins 20261101120000_tenant_admin_group_onboard_select.sql P3-04h (TICK-373, PLAN-003 D-3-3): \"active implies the tenant has an admin\" 20261101130000_entity_versions_published_by_check.sql P3-06c (TICK-383): every version after v1 names who published it, in the 20261101130100_entity_versions_published_by_validate.sql P3-06c (TICK-383): validates 20261101130000's 20261101180000_entity_definition_versions_jsonb_check.sql TICK-378: fixed_columns and states on entity_definition_versions must be 20261101180100_entity_definition_versions_jsonb_check_validate.sql TICK-378: validates the previous migration's fixed_columns and states 20261101190000_records_pipeline.sql P3-09 (TICK-353): records gains what the write pipeline needs, audit_log is 20261102100000_cascade_start.sql P3-12 (TICK-88): cascade_start, the start key of ADR-001 amendment A5 20261102120000_idempotency_response_headers.sql TICK-412: a replayed idempotent response restores an allow-listed set of 20261102120100_idempotency_response_headers_validate.sql TICK-412: validates the previous migration's response_headers CHECK in its 20261102130000_audit_log_v0.sql P5-10a (TICK-427), PLAN-005 D-5-1: S9's interim audit table becomes 20261102130100_tenant_auditor_group.sql P5-18 (TICK-434, PLAN-005 D-5-4): the platform id of each tenant's auditors 20261102233600_audit_log.sql P5-10b (TICK-450): ADR-004's audit_log, its chain head and the pipeline's 20261103100000_audit_commit_check.sql P5-11 (TICK-428): ADR-004 decision 2's commit check, as a function any 20261103120000_audit_backfill_s8.sql P5-15 (TICK-433): migration A's backfill (ADR-004 R25 A half, R26). Every 20261103150000_audit_commit_check_custom_plan.sql P5-11-E3 (TICK-508): audit_commit_check() is planned on every call 20261104013400_groups_tombstone.sql ADR-004-E5 step (i), expand (TICK-534; D1, D2, D5, D10, D11, I1, I5). Group 20261104013500_groups_tombstone_validate.sql ADR-004-E5 step (i) (TICK-534, I5): validates 20261104013400's 20261104043000_groups_contract.sql ADR-004-E5 step (ii-a), contract (TICK-538; D2, D10). Runs only after step 20261104043100_groups_contract_validate.sql ADR-004-E5 step (ii-b) (TICK-538; D10, CI-E5-8): validates 20261104043000's 20261104100000_drop_audit_log_v0.sql P5-17 (TICK-435): drops S9's audit_log_v0 (PLAN-005 D-5-1, moved here from 20261104110000_records_audit_commit_check.sql P5-13c (TICK-530): attach ADR-004 decision 2's commit check to records, 20261104133000_audit_reader_policies.sql ADR-004-A1 A1-3 (TICK-558): nava_audit_reader, the masking reader's own 20261104191200_auditor_group_active_tenant.sql P5-23 (TICK-568, ADR-004 with ADR-001-A6, PLAN-005 D-5-4): nava_onboard may 20261105100000_onboard_seal_start.sql P5-33 (TICK-441, ADR-001-A6.2, ADR-004 R16): onboarding seeds a tenant's 20261107053707_audit_migration_b.sql P5-32 (TICK-442): migration B (ADR-004 R25 B half, R26, R27; R16 20261107100000_audit_chain_head_dead_lineage.sql P5-34f (TICK-590, ADR-004 R17): the dead-lineage gauge. nava-purge, as 20261108120000_record_write_keys_actor.sql TICK-626 (AR-TICK-480-01, carried onto D-5-3): an idempotency key belongs 20261108120100_record_write_keys_actor_key.sql TICK-626 (AR-TICK-480-01): record_write_keys is unique per principal. 20261108124249_acl_tables.sql P3-30 (TICK-615): the S10 authorization tables (PLAN-003 D-3-2, the S10 20261108184658_record_shares_typed_principals.sql P3-30a (TICK-625, closes AR-S10-01): a record share names a principal of 20261109100000_acl_versions_shape_check.sql TICK-629 (deferred from P3-31): acl_versions.rules and masks must have 20261109100100_acl_versions_shape_check_validate.sql TICK-629: validates the previous migration's rules and masks CHECKs in 20261109120000_acl_versions_audit_commit_check.sql P3-35 (TICK-620): attach the audit commit check to acl_versions (ADR-004 20261109130000_audit_log_subject_acl_policy.sql P3-35 (TICK-620), contract amendment A3: registers the audit subject type 20261109130100_audit_log_subject_acl_policy_validate.sql P3-35 (TICK-620): validates the previous migration's audit_log subject_type 20261109140000_records_list_updated.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the default sort (-updated_at) and sort=updated_at. A 20261109140100_records_list_created.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): sort=created_at, read forward (descending) or backward 20261109140200_records_list_state.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): sort=state, ordered state, updated_at, id; and 20261109140300_records_list_group.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for assignment_group_id: 20261109140400_records_list_requester.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for requester_id: filter[requester]=\u003cp\u003e 20261109140500_records_list_assignee.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): the access path for assignee_id: filter[assignee]=\u003cp\u003e and 20261109140600_drop_records_s9_list_indexes.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): drops P3-09's list indexes, which the records_list_* 20261109140700_platform_database_jit_off.sql TICK-640 (P3-34b): JIT off for every session on the platform database. 20261110040000_entity_definitions_numbering_mode.sql P3-36f (TICK-659): each entity type chooses how its records are numbered 20261110040100_entity_definitions_numbering_mode_validate.sql P3-36f (TICK-659): validates entity_definitions_numbering_mode_check, added 20261110100000_records_list_group_state.sql TICK-656 (P3-36d): the group access path with state as an index condition."},{"url":"database/schema.html","title":"Schema snapshot (schema.json)","section":"database","text":"Schema snapshot: schema.json docs/handbook/src/database/schema.json describes the platform database schema as a freshly migrated database has it. The handbook build ( docs/handbook/build/build.py ) reads this file. It never parses the migration SQL and never connects to a database (TICK-718 D-HB-9, TICK-733). This page is the contract between the writer (TICK-733) and the handbook reader (TICK-719), as the Technical Lead decided on TICK-733. Regenerating it The snapshot is generated, never hand-edited. Regenerate it after a migration lands on main , and commit it with the handbook update for that change. Start the local stack ( docker compose up ), so that the migrate one-shot has migrated the platform database nava . The migrator also builds the runtime audit_log policies from db/audited-tables.yaml . From the repository root, run the one command: docker compose exec -T postgres sh -c 'PGPASSWORD=\"$(cat /run/pg_superuser_password)\" psql -h /var/run/postgresql -U pgadmin -d nava -XAtq -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1' \u003c docs/handbook/build/schema.sql | python3 docs/handbook/build/schema_json.py docs/handbook/src/database/schema.json On Windows, use python instead of python3 if that is your interpreter's name. The two build files do the following: docs/handbook/build/schema.sql reads the catalogue and prints one JSON document. Every name is sorted with COLLATE \"C\" (byte order), so the output does not depend on the database's default collation. docs/handbook/build/schema_json.py uses only the Python standard library. It checks the document against the key table below: exact keys per object, kind values, the partition-key rules, and byte order. It refuses a table outside schema public or a foreign table, and it refuses connection details (see the last section). It writes the file only when every check passes. Version 1 The file is UTF-8 with LF line endings, a two-space indent and one trailing newline. Non-ASCII characters are written as-is, not escaped. Every key below is always present: a string that does not apply is \"\" , and a list that is empty is [] . A reader ignores keys it does not know, so a new key is additive and does not change version . Renaming or removing a key bumps version . The snapshot covers schema public only. Tables are named by their bare name. goose_db_version , the migrator's bookkeeping table, is left out. Key Value version 1 . A reader rejects any other value. tables Every table in public , sorted by name . tables[].name The table name. tables[].kind Exactly one of table , partitioned or partition . tables[].partition_of A partition's parent table, else \"\" . tables[].partition_by A partitioned table's key ( pg_get_partkeydef ), for example RANGE (occurred_at) , else \"\" . tables[].partition_bound A partition's bound ( pg_get_expr(relpartbound) ), for example DEFAULT , else \"\" . tables[].rls Row level security flags. tables[].rls.enabled ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. tables[].rls.forced FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY. tables[].columns Columns in ordinal ( attnum ) order. tables[].columns[].name Column name. tables[].columns[].type format_type , for example timestamp with time zone . tables[].columns[].nullable true unless the column is NOT NULL. tables[].columns[].default The deparsed default expression, else \"\" . tables[].primary_key Primary key columns in key order, [] when there is no primary key. tables[].foreign_keys Foreign keys sorted by name . A partition lists its own copies of its parent's. tables[].foreign_keys[].name Constraint name. tables[].foreign_keys[].columns Referencing columns in key order. tables[].foreign_keys[].ref_table Referenced table. tables[].foreign_keys[].ref_columns Referenced columns in key order. tables[].foreign_keys[].on_delete NO ACTION , RESTRICT , CASCADE , SET NULL or SET DEFAULT . tables[].indexes Indexes sorted by name , including the primary key's. A partition lists its own copies of its parent's. tables[].indexes[].name Index name. tables[].indexes[].method Access method, for example btree or gin . tables[].indexes[].unique true for a unique index. tables[].indexes[].columns Key columns in order. An expression index's entry is the deparsed expression. tables[].indexes[].include The INCLUDE columns, [] when there are none. tables[].indexes[].predicate A partial index's deparsed WHERE , else \"\" . tables[].policies pg_policies rows sorted by name . tables[].policies[].name Policy name. tables[].policies[].command SELECT , INSERT , UPDATE , DELETE or ALL . tables[].policies[].roles Roles, sorted. tables[].policies[].permissive true for PERMISSIVE, false for RESTRICTIVE. A RESTRICTIVE policy changes what isolation means, so the sensitivity check must see it. tables[].policies[].using The deparsed USING , else \"\" . tables[].policies[].check The deparsed WITH CHECK , else \"\" . The predicates are PostgreSQL's own deparse, so the tenant predicate reads exactly as catalog.TenantPred(\"tenant_id\") . Not in version 1: check and unique constraints, sequences, functions, triggers, grants, owners, comments and table areas. Grants come from db/grants.yaml , audited or exempt status from db/audited-tables.yaml , and the table-to-area mapping from docs/handbook/src/database/areas.yaml on the handbook side. What it never contains The file holds no DSN, host, port, role password, pg_hba.conf address or local path. Version 1 has no key that could carry one. schema_json.py refuses to write a file that matches any of its leak patterns, and HB-12's manual sensitivity sweep covers the file. A future version that adds a free-text key, such as comments or owners, needs a new sensitivity review first."},{"url":"glossary/index.html","title":"Glossary","section":"glossary","text":"Terms used across the handbook, including every term that appears in a vision-section heading. Each entry points to the page that explains it most fully. Pages for the security, database and workflow sections are written by other handbook tasks; until they exist, entries point to the section's index. Tenancy and identity Tenant. One customer organisation using the shared platform. Main page: I1: tenant wall . Tenant wall. The row-level-security rule set that makes a query without a tenant return nothing. I1 . Row-level security (RLS) and FORCE RLS. A PostgreSQL feature that adds an invisible \"only this tenant's rows\" filter to every query. FORCE applies it even to the table's owner. I1 . Principal. The authenticated caller of an API request, a user or a service, resolved from a verified token inside one tenant. I2 . Group. A set of principals in one tenant, changed only through the platform API (tenants cannot administer Keycloak in Milestone 1, residual R3) and mirrored from Keycloak into the platform's identity tables by the group sync ( internal/groupsync ); used by access rules. I5 . Identity port and adapter. The core-owned interface to the identity provider and the single package that talks to Keycloak. I8 . Platform-verified source. A tenant value that comes from a validated token via the identity binding table, or from a row the platform wrote. I2 . Database roles Database role and least privilege. One narrow database account per system actor; none is a superuser, owns objects or bypasses RLS. I3 . Grant manifest. The single file listing every permission of every role; CI compares the live database with it. I3 . Migrator. The one-shot job that owns the schema and runs migrations. Database . Records and writes Entity definition and entity definition version. A tenant's JSON Schema description of a kind of record, and one published, immutable version of it. Database . Record. One stored item of an entity type, with a version used for optimistic concurrency. I4 . Write pipeline. The single path every write takes: authorisation, defaults, schema validation, checks, commit with audit. I4 . Optimistic concurrency. An update names the version it expects; a stale one is a typed conflict (HTTP 412 with If-Match ). I4 . Authorization layer. The access rules, compiled to SQL and agreeing with the Go single-record check. I5 . ACL and ACL version. An access-control list for an entity type, and the version number of the published list a decision was made under. I5 . Mask (field mask). A rule hiding a field from readers who may not see it, on write and on read. I5 . Numbering mode. How a record's human-readable number is allocated, per tenant, from a counter table. I8 . Idempotency key. A client-supplied key ( Idempotency-Key ) that makes a repeated write return the first response. I7 . Idempotent. Doing it twice has the effect of doing it once. I7 . Audit Audit log. The per-tenant record of every change, written in the same transaction as the change. Database . Seal. The sequence number and hash assigned to an audit entry by the asynchronous sealer. Database . Chain head. The latest sealed entry of a tenant's audit hash chain; each new seal hashes the previous one. Database . Workflows Outbox. A table of work rows saved in the same transaction as the change that caused them. I6 . Relay. The process that reads outbox rows and starts or signals workflows. I6 . Temporal and workflow. The workflow engine and a durable process it runs. Workflow code is deterministic; side effects run in activities. I6 . Causation and cascade. The origin event id and depth carried by every write and outbox row; a cascade is a write that triggers further writes, bounded by a depth limit. I9 . Process words Invariant. A rule that must always hold; changing one needs a new signed ADR. The nine invariants . Decision (D and G numbers). D numbers are decisions in ADR-001 section 2; G numbers are the TICK-2 gaps it closes (G7 is recorded as a decision). Goals and non-goals . Goal and non-goal. What Milestone 1 sets out to do, and what it deliberately leaves out. Goals and non-goals . Configuration, not code. Tenant changes are data, never new code or schema changes. I8 . Advisory AI. AI may suggest, and change data only above a recorded confidence threshold. I8 . Proof and enforcement point. The test or ADR section that shows an invariant holds, and the layer that applies it. The nine invariants . Known technical risks. The CTO's register of live risks. Vision . ADR. Architecture Decision Record. Architecture decisions ."},{"url":"index.html","title":"Nava handbook","section":"home","text":"Orientation for engineers: what the platform is, how it is built, and where each part lives. Every page describes only what is on main ; anything on a story branch is marked \"in progress\". TODO: sections overview and reading order (written by the content tasks of TICK-718). Vision System Architecture decisions Stories Workflows Database Security Benchmarks Operations Glossary"},{"url":"operations/index.html","title":"Operations","section":"operations","text":"TODO: the public API operations and how to run the stack. Audit operations Operation Method and path Summary Responses reseedAuditChain POST /api/v1/audit/chain:reseed Restart a dead audit seal lineage 200, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 429, 500, 501 listAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries The tenant's audit timeline 200, 400, 401, 403, 429, 500, 501 exportAuditEntries GET /api/v1/audit/entries:export Download a verifiable JSONL export of the audit log 200, 400, 401, 403, 409, 429, 500, 501"},{"url":"security/index.html","title":"Security","section":"security","text":"TODO: tenant isolation, authentication and the threat model."},{"url":"stories/index.html","title":"Stories","section":"stories","text":"TODO: one page per delivered story, from the story manifest."},{"url":"system/index.html","title":"System","section":"system","text":"TODO: components and how a request flows through them. See also the viewer sample ."},{"url":"system/viewer-sample.html","title":"Viewer sample","section":"system","text":"The viewer gives every diagram zoom, pan, reset and full screen. Authors write nothing extra. Remove this page when real diagrams exist. Mermaid Excalidraw Search target The word flamingo appears only on this page, so search can be checked."},{"url":"vision/bounded-cascades.html","title":"I9: Cascades are bounded","section":"vision","text":"I9 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Every write and every outbox row carries a causation record: the id of the origin event and a depth. The pipeline rejects a write beyond a hard, platform-wide depth limit, and rejects a cycle (the same origin seen again on the same record), with a typed error and an audit entry. The limit is platform configuration, never tenant configuration. Where it is enforced The write pipeline ( internal/records/pipeline ) and the outbox ( internal/outbox ), which records causation_id and causation_depth . Proof Annex B CI-10 (a cascade beyond the limit, or a cycle, is rejected with a typed error and audited). The cycle-suppression rule for workflow starts is amendment ADR-001-A5. Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestCI10_DepthBeyondLimitRejectedAndAuditedOnTheRunner , TestCI10_DepthBeyondLimitWritesNothing , TestCI10_SiblingAndSelfRetriggeringWritesAreNotCycles , TestCreateRecordIgnoresCausationFromTheRequest . Read next Enforcement detail: Workflows and Database . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/contracts-configuration-advisory-ai.html","title":"I8: Contracts are the boundary; configuration, not code; AI is advisory","section":"vision","text":"I8 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Tenant code, plugins and AI reach data only through the capability contract or the public API, with a scoped token. They never reach the database or Temporal directly. A tenant change never needs a schema change, with one exception: an operator-approved, measured and audited indexed attribute, run by the migrator outside the tenant's change set. Per-tenant numbering uses a counter table, not sequences. AI may change data only above an explicit confidence threshold, and the decision is recorded. Where it is enforced Ports and adapters in the Go packages (the identity port is the worked example, internal/identity ), a depguard import rule in .golangci.yml , and the numbering allocator. Proof Annex B CI-11 is a static import and lint check, not a Go test: only the identity adapter package may import a Keycloak client. The contract suite TestContract runs the adapter against the port's contract. No test on main covers the AI confidence threshold: no AI code that writes data is on main , so that clause has nothing to prove yet. Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestContract , TestCompileNumberingMode , TestAllocationTransactionIsTenantScoped . Read next Enforcement detail: Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/durable-path-outbox.html","title":"I6: The durable path is deterministic, versioned and entered only through the outbox","section":"vision","text":"I6 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Workflow code is deterministic: all I/O, clocks, randomness and tenant code run in activities. A running execution keeps the version it started on. Nothing starts or signals a workflow except the relay, dispatching an outbox row that was committed in the same transaction as the change that caused it. Where it is enforced The transactional outbox ( internal/outbox ), the relay ( internal/relay ), the workflow interpreter ( internal/workflow ) and the Temporal Authorizer, which refuses every other caller ( internal/temporalstack ). Proof Annex B CI-6 (relay start and signal behaviour, certificate and command checks) and CI-7 (payloads carry references only). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestDemoOutboxTwiceWithOneKeyIsOneWorkflowWithTwoSignalsInOrder , TestCI6_RelayRedeliveredStartAfterCompletionStartsNoSecondRun , TestCI7_ProducerSchemaRejectsRecordDataFields . Read next Enforcement detail: Workflows . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/goals-and-non-goals.html","title":"Goals and non-goals","section":"vision","text":"This page follows ADR-001 and the README, and the Milestone 1 scope as recorded in the ADR-001 reading guide. It adds no goals of its own. Goals Keep tenants apart. One tenant can never read or change another's data. The nine invariants are the rules that guarantee it. Know the tenant from a verified source only (decision D26): the identity provider's token, resolved through a platform lookup table. One predictable API (decision D6): REST at /api/v1 , generated from an OpenAPI contract, with stable error codes, idempotency keys and optimistic concurrency. Reliable background work (decision D22): saved changes start workflows through a transactional outbox and a relay, with workflows kept inside their tenant. Narrow database accounts (decision D28): one account per system actor, none able to skip the tenant wall. Third-party systems behind ports and adapters , so a vendor can be replaced without touching the core (README, design principles). Non-goals for Milestone 1 ADR-001 and its reading guide list what is deliberately left out, because each item adds a path that is not yet secured: Temporal Schedules, cron and reset. Partitioned tables. Tenant self-service administration of the identity provider (residual R3); the platform API does it. GraphQL. It arrives later, as a read surface over the same authorization layer. Encrypting workflow histories per tenant (residual R1). Workflow payloads carry references only. Planned, not on main Stories S12, S18, S19 and S20 are planned and are not described anywhere in this handbook yet; the stories section lists them."},{"url":"vision/idempotent-side-effects.html","title":"I7: Every side effect is idempotent","section":"vision","text":"I7 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says A retry, a replay or a redelivery never acts twice. A repeated request with the same idempotency key returns the same response; the same key with a different body is a conflict. A redelivered workflow start or signal is applied once. Where it is enforced The idempotency middleware and the pipeline ( internal/platform/idempotency , internal/records/pipeline ), the outbox dedupe key ( internal/outbox ) and the relay's reject-duplicate start ( internal/relay ). Proof Annex B CI-8 (idempotency replay and mismatch) and CI-6 (redelivered start and signal). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestIdempotencyKeyReplays , TestIdempotencyKeyMismatchConflicts , TestCI10_DuplicateDedupeKeyWritesOneRow , TestCI6_RelayRedeliveredSignalAppliedOnceAcrossContinueAsNew . Read next Enforcement detail: Workflows and Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/index.html","title":"Vision","section":"vision","text":"What the product is The platform ( nava-core ) is a multi-tenant ticketing platform. Many customer organisations, called tenants, share one installation, one database and one set of services. The main risk of that design is one tenant seeing or changing another tenant's data, so the architecture is built around keeping tenants apart. ADR-001 records the ground rules; see goals and non-goals . Who uses it Tenant users and applications call the REST API at /api/v1 with a token issued by the identity provider. Tenant administrators publish entity types, set access rules and manage users and groups through the platform API. Platform operators onboard tenants, run migrations and administer the Temporal workflow engine. Engineers and the product owner build and accept the platform; this handbook is for them. Read next Goals and non-goals The nine invariants , one page each: I1 , I2 , I3 , I4 , I5 , I6 , I7 , I8 , I9 Glossary Known technical risks , kept by the CTO outside the handbook"},{"url":"vision/invariants.html","title":"The nine invariants","section":"vision","text":"An invariant is a rule that must always hold. ADR-001 section 1 lists nine, I1 to I9. Later designs cannot break one without a new ADR signed by the product owner. Each page below says the rule in plain words, names the layer that enforces it and lists the tests that prove it. ID Invariant I1 Tenant wall in the database I2 The tenant comes only from a platform-verified source I3 Least-privilege database roles I4 One write pipeline with optimistic concurrency I5 One authorization layer, inside the query I6 The durable path is deterministic, versioned and entered only through the outbox I7 Every side effect is idempotent I8 Contracts are the boundary; configuration, not code; AI is advisory I9 Cascades are bounded Decisions that are not invariants Decision G7 (one shared database, shared tables, forced row-level security, tenant_id leading every tenant-table index) is recorded as a decision, not an invariant. Moving a large tenant to its own database is an operator option. Isolation does not depend on the topology: I1 and I3 keep it. Known technical risks The known technical risks are tracked in the CTO's register, outside the handbook."},{"url":"vision/least-privilege-roles.html","title":"I3: Least-privilege database roles","section":"vision","text":"I3 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says No account the platform runs as is a superuser, can skip row-level security, owns an object, or is a member of any other role. Nothing is granted to PUBLIC , including through default privileges. Every permission comes from one manifest, and CI compares the live database with it. No SECURITY DEFINER function or policy-bypassing view is reachable from a runtime account. Where it is enforced The one-shot migrator and the roles job ( internal/db ), the grants manifest db/grants.yaml , and the catalogue checks run against a live database. Proof Annex B CI-3 (role membership and ownership), CI-4 (privileges equal the manifest exactly) and CI-9 (each system actor can do only its own job). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestD28NoSuperuserOrBypassRLS , TestManifestDetectsAndReportsGrantDrift , TestDBRolesNewPlatformDatabaseHasNoPublicGrants , TestT10GateEmptySecurityDefinerExceptions . Read next Enforcement detail: Database and Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/one-authorization-layer.html","title":"I5: One authorization layer, inside the query","section":"vision","text":"I5 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Access rules are compiled into SQL, and the single-record check in Go gives the same answer as the SQL. Field masks apply on write and on read. A record the caller cannot read, a record of another tenant and a record that does not exist all produce the same response. Where it is enforced The ACL compiler and checker ( internal/authz ) used by the records pipeline and the list queries ( internal/records/pipeline , internal/server ). Proof Annex B CI-8 (byte-identical 404s, a masked-field filter returns 400) and the I5 agreement suite in internal/tenancy/isolation . Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestI5Agreement , TestCompileReadAgreesWithAllows , TestRecordFourNotFoundCasesAreByteIdentical , TestMaskedFieldInFilterIsRefusedBeforeSQL . Read next Enforcement detail: Security . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/one-write-pipeline.html","title":"I4: One write pipeline with optimistic concurrency","section":"vision","text":"I4 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Every writer goes through the same steps: authorisation, defaults and computed values, JSON Schema validation, declarative checks, then commit with the audit entry in one transaction. Every update carries the version of the record it expects. A write against a stale version is a typed conflict, never last-writer-wins. Where it is enforced The records pipeline ( internal/records/pipeline ), reached from the API handlers in internal/server . An architecture test keeps record writers inside the pipeline. Proof Annex B CI-8 (a stale If-Match returns 412) and the architecture tests in internal/records/arch_test.go . Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestEveryWriteBumpsVersionOnce , TestIfMatchVersions , TestRecordWritersOnlyInPipeline , TestPipelineImportRule . Read next Enforcement detail: Security and Database . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/tenant-source.html","title":"I2: The tenant comes only from a platform-verified source","section":"vision","text":"I2 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says The tenant of a request is never taken from anything the caller supplies. It comes from a validated token, resolved through the platform's identity binding table (decision D26), or from a row the platform itself wrote under row-level security (the outbox row, decision D22). Request bodies, payloads, script arguments, workflow inputs, signal bodies and search attributes are never a source. Temporal is a path like any other. Where it is enforced The identity port and its Keycloak adapter ( internal/identity , internal/identity/keycloak ) resolve the token to a tenant; the API layer passes only that value on; the relay reads the tenant from the outbox row; the Temporal Authorizer checks workflow ids and commands ( internal/temporalstack ). Proof Annex B CI-5 (a catalogue of rejected tokens: zero or two organizations, unknown issuer, wrong-realm key, ID token as access token, foreign IdP, duplicate binding) and CI-6 (Temporal). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestCI5 , TestClaimsForIgnoresClientTenant , TestGetMeIgnoresSuppliedTenant , TestCI6_WorkerWrongTenantSignalDroppedWithAlertWorkflowKeepsRunning . Read next Enforcement detail: Security and Workflows . All nine invariants: the invariants page ."},{"url":"vision/tenant-wall.html","title":"I1: Tenant wall in the database","section":"vision","text":"I1 is one of the nine invariants in ADR-001 section 1. The wording below follows that table, paraphrased for readability; the ADR is the decision. What it says Every table that holds tenant data carries a tenant_id , has row-level security enabled and forced , and has a policy that compares tenant_id with the tenant the session declared. If a session declares no tenant, a query returns no rows or an error, never another tenant's rows. Unique keys and foreign keys include tenant_id . A policy that does not test the tenant must be on a short, named allow-list. Where it is enforced The database itself: PostgreSQL row-level security on every tenant table ( db/migrations ), plus the per-request tenant setting made by internal/tenancy and internal/db . Proof ADR-001 Annex B CI-1 (catalogue checks) and CI-2 (no tenant set gives zero rows or an error, for every runtime role and every tenant table). Tests that prove it, by Go test name: TestCI2EveryRoleEveryTenantTable , TestT11TenantTablesFailClosed , TestT09PolicyAllowList . Read next Enforcement detail: Database . All nine invariants: the invariants page . Note The shared database and shared tables are decision G7 , not an invariant: topology is a performance and deployment choice. I1 and I3 are what keep tenants apart, whatever the topology."},{"url":"workflows/index.html","title":"Workflows","section":"workflows","text":"TODO: the durable workflows and their activities."}];
